    {
        "content": "<p>I think the issue with the approaches by <code>solve_by_elim</code> or instance declarations, is that the lemmas involved (e.g. <code>IsScalarTower.to₁₂₄</code>) require an additional type <code>D</code> that doesn't appear in the goal <code>IsScalarTower A B C</code> itself and Lean has no way to infer <code>D</code> by unification, so I don't think they can work on their own. This is why I think we need a tactic that explicitly enumerates the types available in the local context and tries them as candidates for these intermediate arguments.</p>",
        "id": 577167485,
        "sender_full_name": "Xavier Roblot",
        "timestamp": 1772615905
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can use grind for those.</p>",
        "id": 577170545,
        "sender_full_name": "Xavier Généreux",
        "timestamp": 1772616762
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't know much about the way <code>grind</code> works... But I can have a look if you think it can help</p>",
        "id": 577171757,
        "sender_full_name": "Xavier Roblot",
        "timestamp": 1772617094
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I experimented with tagging the relevant lemmas with <code>grind</code>. It does succeed when one use of <code>IsScalarTower.to*</code> is enough to close the goal but fails when more than one is needed.</p>",
        "id": 577254659,
        "sender_full_name": "Xavier Roblot",
        "timestamp": 1772639603
    }
